WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
INTRO:
Facebook, Apple, Amazon, Netflix, and Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.080
These 5 companies have a reputation for paying&nbsp;
some of the most exorbitant salaries and offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.080
the best perks in the world atleast to tech&nbsp;
workers. Google for example has a median salary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:24.800
$295,000 per year while Facebook comes in at&nbsp;
$292,000 per year. And that’s just the median.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.680
If you have any sort of seniority within these&nbsp;
companies, you’ll be making closer to $500,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:36.800
per year if not more. 20 years ago, these types of&nbsp;
salaries were only offered to C suite executives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.760
but these tech companies have made them&nbsp;
more common than ever. Aside from these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.640
insane pay packages, many tech companies&nbsp;
also have generous work from home policies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.480
and unlimited vacation time. Considering these&nbsp;
extraordinary perks, working at these companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:59.040
is the top career goal for many, and getting a job&nbsp;
at one of these companies is extremely difficult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.200
At Google, for instance, the chances of&nbsp;
scoring a job for the average applicant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.960
is just 0.2%. Hearing this, you would think&nbsp;
that the people who make it to the other side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.200
would be extremely loyal and stick around for&nbsp;
decades. But, this is actually not the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.600
In fact, these companies not only rank at the top&nbsp;
of the salary lists, but also at the top of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:28.000
turnover lists. The average tenure of employees at&nbsp;
these companies is usually no more than 2 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:34.320
Now, it should be noted that this study measures&nbsp;
tenure and not turnover, so it is a bit skewed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.720
These companies are always hiring new&nbsp;
employees and growing their workforces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:44.800
so naturally, the average tenure is going to skew&nbsp;
towards 0. With that being said though, it’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.840
secret that these companies have high turnover. If&nbsp;
you know people within these companies, you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.680
that most of them jump around every few years, and&nbsp;
this is the same case with most of their coworkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.280
as well. Not to mention the thousands of YouTube&nbsp;
videos and reddit posts about quitting FAANG.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:07.840
So, why in the world do the best places&nbsp;
to work also have the highest turnover?

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.280
FINANCIAL FREEDOM:&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.240
One of the top reasons many FAANG engineers&nbsp;
aren’t enticed to stay at their jobs is because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.440
the salary isn’t that big of a deal for them.&nbsp;
This isn’t to say that they’re ungrateful for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.240
opportunities that they have or that they don’t&nbsp;
care about money. When they entered the industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.080
they probably very much cared about the high&nbsp;
compensation and cool perks. However, by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:38.240
they’re ten years into the industry, most of them&nbsp;
simply achieve financial freedom. Now, they don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.480
become centimillionaires or billionaires, but they&nbsp;
almost always become multi-millionaires if not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.800
deca-millionaires. But, wait a minute, even if&nbsp;
you’re getting paid $300,000 per year, this only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:56.240
comes out to $3 million within 10 years. And by&nbsp;
the time you deal with California’s taxes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.680
living expenses, you’d be lucky to be left with&nbsp;
a million dollars. So, how do these guys manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:08.360
to save up over $5 million+ or $10 million+? Well,&nbsp;
the answer lies in stock compensation. Even entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:14.320
level software engineers at Facebook earn $42,000&nbsp;
worth of stock compensation every single year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.200
You just have to get promoted once for this&nbsp;
number to nearly double to $81,000 per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.400
By the time you get to your 2nd or 3rd promotion,&nbsp;
you’re stock compensation itself is worth six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:31.280
figures if not multiple six figures. Also, it’s&nbsp;
not like this money is being invested into a 401K&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:37.600
or the S&amp;P 500, this money is literally being&nbsp;
invested into the fastest growing companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:45.360
in the world. Over the past 10 years, Facebook&nbsp;
grew 21X, Amazon grew 21X, Apple grew 13X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:52.080
Netflix grew 90X, and Google grew 10X. Now, let’s&nbsp;
say you ended up at the slowest grower which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.640
Google in 2012, and that you decide to stick&nbsp;
around for 10 years. Maybe, you start at level 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.120
and make it level 5. This would mean that you’re&nbsp;
average stock compensation during this time period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:11.920
would be $83,000 per year. Even if we estimate&nbsp;
conservatively and call it $70,000 per year. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:16.480
let’s also say that you’re a financial idiot and&nbsp;
that you live paycheck to paycheck, you don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:22.800
contribute to your 401K, and you have no savings.&nbsp;
As long as you don’t sell your stock compensation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:29.360
you would enjoy an annual growth rate of&nbsp;
27.8%, and you’d be left with $2.6 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.400
by the end of 10 years, and that’s the worst&nbsp;
case scenario. If you were to do the same thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.680
at Netflix which was the fastest growing of the&nbsp;
bunch, you’d enjoy an annual growth rate of 61%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:46.080
which would leave you with $13.3 million. If&nbsp;
you jumped around every couple of years like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.480
most of these guys do, you probably would’ve&nbsp;
ended somewhere in between those two numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:55.760
likely $5 to $10 million and that’s just the&nbsp;
stock compensation. If you also take advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:01.200
of these companies’ 401K plans and invest in&nbsp;
the S&amp;P 500 yourself, you can easily boast a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.720
net worth of over $10 million by the time you’re&nbsp;
in your mid 30s. When you’re in such a position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.080
earning more money gives you basically 0 tangible&nbsp;
value. People who are still pursuing wealth after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.440
this point are simply playing the money&nbsp;
game and seeing how high they can get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.040
But, in terms of daily life, earning more money&nbsp;
gives them no additional value and they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:27.520
retire at any time, so it’s no wonder why FAANG&nbsp;
salaries are not enough to retain these employees.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.760
MONEY DRIVEN:&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.880
As you can see, the vast majority of FAANG&nbsp;
employees become financially free at a very young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.200
age, so they’re not driven to stay at a company&nbsp;
just for the salary. But, even if they were driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.480
by the salary, it would still be in their best&nbsp;
interest to not stay at the same company. Here’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:54.640
the thing, when you stay at the same company, at&nbsp;
best, you’ll earn a 3-5% raise every year. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:59.600
switch companies strategically, however, you can&nbsp;
squeeze in a 10-20% raise every couple of years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.040
And when you do this for a prolonged period of&nbsp;
time, the person who constantly switched jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.520
ends up earning a lot more than the loyal&nbsp;
employee. According to Forbes, employees who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:15.840
stay at companies for longer than 2 years are paid&nbsp;
50% less on average. Now, this isn’t to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:22.320
you should switch every 24 months because that’ll&nbsp;
likely become a red flag very quickly. But, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:28.160
in your best interest to switch periodically as in&nbsp;
every 5 to 7 years and this is especially true in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.360
the FAANG world. While it’s extremely difficult to&nbsp;
get into FAANG, once you’re in and have a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.720
of years under your belt, it’s comically easy to&nbsp;
switch between these companies. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.600
the reason acceptance rates at FAANG companies are&nbsp;
so low is not because these companies don’t need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:49.440
more talent. These companies are always desperate&nbsp;
for more talent, and the reason acceptance rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:54.000
are so low is because it’s extremely difficult&nbsp;
to find employees that are at the caliber that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.440
they’re looking for. But, if you’ve already passed&nbsp;
the screening process of one of their competitors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.560
they can be pretty confident that you’ll be a good&nbsp;
hire. FAANG companies are infamous for pouching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.680
employees from competitors. In fact, they’re&nbsp;
almost always embroiled in a bunch of lawsuits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.920
regarding stealing employees and intellectual&nbsp;
property and things along those lines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.200
So, as top tier FAANG talent, you can use this&nbsp;
rivalry amongst these companies to maximize your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.640
own compensation. Something else to note is that&nbsp;
switching to other FAANG companies in not their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:30.320
only option. In many cases, they go onto found&nbsp;
their own companies. And given that they these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:35.920
guys have intelligence, experience, and networks,&nbsp;
the odds are very much stacked in their favor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.600
This is why companies like Xiaomi, Pinterest,&nbsp;
Shift Technologies, Upstart, and YouTube were all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:48.000
founded by ex silicon valley employees. Imagine if&nbsp;
Eric Yuan stayed at Cisco instead of founding Zoom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.280
or if Reid Hoffman stayed at PayPal instead of&nbsp;
founding LinkedIn. More times than not, these guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.120
have their eyes on targets that are much larger&nbsp;
than just $10 to $15 million. Oftentimes, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.880
looking for hundreds of millions if not billions,&nbsp;
so they’re not gonna stick around at Google just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:09.760
so they can get a few hundred thousand dollars&nbsp;
and free food. And given that FAANG recruits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:14.560
the absolute best talent in the world, it’s not&nbsp;
surprising that many of their employees have such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:20.160
ambitions. For most of them, it’s just a matter&nbsp;
of time until they outgrow their roles at FAANG.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:26.960
BURNOUT:
While larger goals and financial freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.560
often play a factor in FAANG employees jumping&nbsp;
ship, these are not the only reasons. Oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.600
it just comes down to employees burning out and&nbsp;
either wanting to take it slow or get a fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:43.040
start. We commonly hear about how great it is&nbsp;
to work at these companies, but a lot of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:48.080
is simply because that’s how these companies want&nbsp;
us to perceive them. All of these companies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.480
master marketers when it comes to selling their&nbsp;
products, and it’s no different when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:58.240
recruiting. They want us to think that their jobs&nbsp;
are the absolute best when it reality, their jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.360
are actually super stressful. But, wait a minute,&nbsp;
don’t FAANG companies have extremely generous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:08.960
vacation policies and paid time off and things&nbsp;
like that? Well yes, they do, but here’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.880
cool thing. These companies don’t have to be the&nbsp;
bad guys to cultivate an extremely competitive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:20.160
stressful working environment. They don’t need to&nbsp;
implement cutthroat managers and directors or hang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:25.360
scary deadlines over your head. When you place&nbsp;
thousands of the smartest and most competitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.160
individuals together, it’s only natural&nbsp;
that they’re gonna try to outdo each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:35.440
You don’t need to scare them or threaten them to&nbsp;
work hard and not take vacations. These guys are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:41.360
naturally not going to take vacations because they&nbsp;
want to shine. They want that next promotion, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:47.440
want that higher status, they want to boost their&nbsp;
egos. They all have their own selfish reasons for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:52.480
working hard and they’re all high motivated&nbsp;
which works out perfectly for these companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.200
The companies can be the good guys offering great&nbsp;
salaries and awesome perks while the employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:02.240
end up working 60 hours per week or more by&nbsp;
themselves. Many of these guys thrive under these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.840
working conditions and they end up as a VP at a&nbsp;
FAANG company or a C suite executive at a startup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:13.120
but many also burn themselves out and end up&nbsp;
scaling back or switching companies. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:18.880
why most employees at Apple report struggling with&nbsp;
burnout and why almost half of Apple and Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:24.160
employees don’t have a close friend at work.&nbsp;
This is also why only about half of employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.280
at most FAANG companies are actually happy at&nbsp;
their jobs. The only exception to this rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:33.840
is Netflix, and the reasons they have such happy&nbsp;
employees is because they lean into a contractor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:39.200
model that turns over employees even faster. So,&nbsp;
most of their employees never even get past the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.640
honeymoon phase. Personally, I’ve seen this play&nbsp;
out in real life. In high school, I had a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.440
science teacher from FAANG and a coach that was&nbsp;
from the NFL. While both of these guys were from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:54.160
completely different industries, I think their&nbsp;
reasoning for taking such a massive paycut were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:11:00.080
identical. They were simply burnt out from always&nbsp;
having to perform at their peak ability. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.440
I should also mention that the computer science&nbsp;
teacher has since returned to FAANG, so clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:10.880
these guys can never truly suppress their inner&nbsp;
urge to constantly progress in their careers. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:16.800
at the same time, they’re never truly satisfied&nbsp;
at any one of these jobs. They’re constantly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.560
this limbo state where they’re hopping jobs every&nbsp;
few years perpetually trying to find inner peace.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.800
THE TRUTH ABOUT FAANG:&nbsp;

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:32.080
In the end, while FAANG companies love trying to&nbsp;
convince us that they’re the best places to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:38.800
it’s simply not true. Job satisfaction is usually&nbsp;
not derived from high salaries are fancy perks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:42.720
Virtually all the jobs with the highest&nbsp;
satisfaction have something to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:48.000
helping others whether that be providing&nbsp;
educational services or therapy. And at FAANG,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:53.120
all you’re usually doing is making the biggest&nbsp;
companies in the world even bigger. There’s also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:58.880
the whole ethical and moral dilemma as well.&nbsp;
Google and Facebook are notorious for privacy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.480
concerns, and as an engineer at these companies,&nbsp;
you’re directly supporting that. Now, none of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:05.840
this is to say that you shouldn’t pursue a job at&nbsp;
FAANG. FAANG is an outstanding place to build your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:11.840
career and achieve financial freedom. But, what&nbsp;
FAANG isn’t is a place to achieve long term zen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:18.160
And that’s why turnover at FAANG is so high. Would&nbsp;
you leave a job at FAANG? Comment that down below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

